 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_kGrd4J3kVJVyMPtJ",
   "name": "Length of an Arc",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Length of an arc"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is an arc?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- An arc is a part of the **circumference **of a circle\n\n  - It is easiest to think of it as the crust of a single slice of pizza\n- The length of an arc depends of the size of the angle at the centre of the circle\n- If the angle at the centre is **less than 180°** then the arc is known as a **minor arc**\n\n  - This could be considered as the crust of a single slice of pizza\n- If the angle at the centre is **more than 180°** then the arc is known as a **major arc**\n\n  - This could be considered as the crust of the remaining pizza after a slice has been taken away"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the length of an arc?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The length of an arc is simply a fraction of the circumference of a circle\n\n  - The fraction can be found by dividing the angle at the centre by 360°\n- The formula for the length, $l$, of an arc is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$l=\\frac{θ}{360}\\times2πr$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where $θ$ is the angle measured in degrees\n\n  - $r$ is the radius"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use radians to find the length of an arc?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- As the radian measure for a **full turn** is $2π$, the fraction of the circle becomes $\\frac{θ}{2π}$\n- Working in radians, the formula for the length of an arc will become"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$l=\\frac{θ}{2π}\\times2πr$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Simplifying, the formula for the length, $l$, of an arc is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$l=rθ$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- - $θ$ is the angle measured in **radians**\n  - $r$ is the radius"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A circular pizza has had a slice cut from it, the angle of the slice that was cut was $\\frac{π}{6}$ rad.\n\nThe radius of the pizza is 12 cm. Find\n\ni) the length of the outside crust of the slice of pizza (the minor arc),\n\n> *A simple diagram will help*\n\n![minor arc diagram for worked example](assets/d0a9ac73a79f-zivsveyx-arcs-we-diagram-1.png)\n\n> *  The formula for the length of an arc, where the angle is in radians is *$s=rθ$\n\n$s=12\\times\\frac{π}{6}$\n\n**Final answer:** **  **$2π\\mathrm{cm}$\n\nii) the perimeter of the remaining pizza.\n\n> *A diagram will help consider where the perimeter is*\n\n![major arc diagram for worked example](assets/1d1a7751e865-idmzejxc-arcs-we-diagram-2.png)\n\n> *Find the angle for the major arc, by subtracting from the angle in a full circle*\n\n$2π-\\frac{π}{6}=\\frac{11π}{6}$\n\n> *  Use the formula for the length of an arc, *$s=rθ$*, to find the curved length of the perimeter, the major arc* $M$\n\n$M=12\\times\\frac{11π}{6}=22π$\n\n> *  As we are finding the perimeter of the whole shape, we need to add on the two straight lengths formed by the slice which has been cut out  *\n\n$22π+12+12$\n\n**  **$22π+24\\mathrm{cm}$** **\n**Unless asked to otherwise, it is best to give answers in an exact form**"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_NfwGSdrHSQtd7Hm9",
   "name": "Area of a Sector",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Area of a sector"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a sector?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A sector is a part of a circle enclosed by two radii (radiuses) and an arc\n\n  - It is easier to think of this as the shape of a single slice of pizza\n- The area of a sector depends of the size of the angle at the centre of the sector\n- If the angle at the centre is **less than 180°** then the sector is known as a **minor sector**\n\n  - This could be considered as the shape of a single slice of pizza\n- If the angle at the centre is **more than 180°** then the sector is known as a **major sector**\n\n  - This could be considered as the shape of the remaining pizza after a slice has been taken away"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the area of a sector?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The area of a sector is simply a fraction of the area of the whole circle\n\n  - The fraction can be found by dividing the angle at the centre by 360°\n- The formula for the area, $A$, of a sector is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$A=\\frac{θ}{360}\\timesπr^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where $θ$ is the angle measured in degrees\n\n  - $r$ is the radius"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use radians to find the area of a sector?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- As the radian measure for a **full turn (360°)** is $2π$, the fraction of the circle becomes $\\frac{θ}{2π}$\n- Working in radians, the formula for the area of a sector will become"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$A=\\frac{θ}{2π}\\timesπr^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Simplifying, the formula for the area, $A$, of a sector is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$A=\\frac{1}{2}r^{2}θ$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $θ$ is the angle measured in **radians**\n\n  - $r$ is the radius"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- These formulae are **not** given to you - you need to remember them!\n- Make sure that you read the question carefully to determine exactly what you need to calculate\n\n  - the arc length or area of a sector,\n  - the perimeter or area of a compound shape,\n  - or something else that incorporates the arc length or area!"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A sector of radius 6 cm has an area of 30 cm<sup>2</sup>. Find the angle at the centre of the sector in radians.   *Draw a diagram to help*\n\n![sector area diagram for worked example](assets/0cb3d665403d-2abgitrm-sectors-we-diagram-1.png)\n\n> *Use the formula for the area of a sector, where the angle is in radians;* $A=\\frac{1}{2}r^{2}θ$\n\n$30=\\frac{1}{2}\\times6^{2}\\timesθ$\n\n> *  Solve for *$θ$*   *\n\n$θ=\\frac{30\\times2}{6^{2}}$\n\n$\\frac{5}{3}\\mathrm{radians}$"
  }
 ]
}